{{#extend "layout"}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 4 &middot; layouts and partials</p>
    <h1>The bar under the arch</h1>
    <p>
      Two pages, one layout, five partials. Nothing on this page repeats markup that
      another page already owns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card">
      {{!-- Chosen by the model, not by this template: swap the value in the
            build script and a different board renders here. --}}
      {{> (lookup model.partials 'board')}}
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      {{> "sourcing"}}
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="source-panel">
    <h2>How this example works</h2>
    <p>
      The page you are reading is only the middle of a document.
      <code>layouts/layout.hbs</code> holds the shell and marks four holes with
      <code>block</code>; this page fills them with <code>content</code>. Blocks can carry
      default markup, so the layout still renders when a page ignores one — the
      <em>House notes</em> section below started as the layout's own text and this page
      appended to it rather than replacing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Partials come in three flavours and all three are on this page:
      <code>partials/site/header.hbs</code> and <code>site/footer.hbs</code> are Handlebars,
      <code>partials/hours.html</code> is plain HTML, and <code>partials/sourcing.md</code>
      is Markdown that kiss renders as it registers it — and which still reads the page's
      model. The price board is a <em>dynamic</em> partial: the template does not name it,
      the model does.
    </p>
    <details open>
      <summary><code>{{config.script.file}}</code></summary>
      <pre><code>{{config.script.text}}</code></pre>
    </details>
  </section>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "aside"}}
  <p>
    This aside replaced the layout's default paragraph outright.
    <a href="stockists.html">The stockists page</a> leaves it alone, so you can see the
    default there.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "notes" mode="append"}}
  <p class="meta">
    Appended by the home page, after the layout's own opening hours — that is
    <code>mode="append"</code> rather than a straight replace.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
